ADS-014 — Lifecycle Model

Document ID: ADS-014
Title: Lifecycle Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Lifecycle Model (CLM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Lifecycle Model establishes a standardized operational lifecycle for all Canonical Atlas Objects (CAOs), ensuring that every object progresses through a governed, auditable, and traceable sequence of states from creation to retirement.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Lifecycle architecture
Lifecycle states
State transitions
Transition governance
Lifecycle inheritance
Lifecycle policies
Quality gates
Retirement model

This specification applies to every Canonical Atlas Object.

3. Canonical Definition

A Lifecycle is the governed sequence of operational states through which a Canonical Atlas Object progresses during its existence.

Lifecycle describes operational maturity.

It does not describe version evolution.

4. Objectives

The Lifecycle Model SHALL:

Standardize operational states.
Enable governance.
Support auditability.
Support approval workflows.
Preserve traceability.
Support automation.
Support compliance.
Support long-term preservation.
5. Lifecycle Principles

Every Atlas object SHALL follow these principles.

Explicit State

Every object SHALL possess exactly one current lifecycle state.

Controlled Transitions

State changes SHALL occur only through authorized transitions.

Auditability

Every transition SHALL be recorded.

Governance

Lifecycle progression SHALL follow governance policies.

Historical Preservation

Historical lifecycle states SHALL remain reconstructable.

Technology Independence

Lifecycle semantics SHALL remain independent of implementation.

6. Canonical Lifecycle States

Atlas defines the following lifecycle states.

State	Description
Proposed	Object concept exists
Draft	Object under development
Validated	Technical validation completed
Reviewed	Governance review completed
Approved	Officially accepted
Published	Available for operational use
Active	Currently in operational use
Suspended	Temporarily unavailable
Superseded	Replaced by another object
Archived	Preserved for historical purposes
Retired	Permanently withdrawn

Future lifecycle profiles SHALL specialize these states without changing their semantics.

